<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=cell-theory">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Science and discovery</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cell theory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1839">1839</a>–<a href="/years/1855">1855</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Cell theory is the scientific theory that living organisms are made up of cells, that the cell is their basic unit, and that all cells come from pre-existing cells; it was first formulated in 1839 by Matthias Jakob Schleiden for plants and Theodor Schwann for animals. It built on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">microscope</a>, with which Robert Hooke had first described cells, in cork, in 1665. Rudolf Virchow added the third tenet, &quot;Omnis cellula e cellula&quot;, in 1855, completing the classical theory, though Robert Remak had already proposed the idea.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cell-theory.jpg" alt="Cell theory" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Robert Hooke (1635–1707) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:RobertHookeMicrographia1665.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1839</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Matthias Jakob Schleiden · Theodor Schwann</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Schleiden claimed as his own an idea Barthélemy Dumortier had stated earlier, and Rudolf Virchow’s third tenet of 1855 had already been proposed by Robert Remak.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/microscope">Microscope<span>c. 1620</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=cell-theory">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
